AVOCADO AGRICULTURE RESEARCH DATA
Verified Numeric Insights from Peer-Reviewed Studies
================================================================================

TEMPERATURE THRESHOLDS
================================================================================

Optimal Temperature Ranges (Hass Variety):
- Vegetative growth optimal: 18–25°C
- Flowering optimal: 18–25°C
- Fruit set optimal: 18–25°C
- Critical minimum (vegetative): 0°C
- Critical maximum (vegetative): 35°C

Specific Temperature Sensitivities:
- Heat stress threshold: >35°C
- Mature tree cold tolerance: −4°C (short duration, no flowers/fruit present)
- Flowering disruption (Type B varieties): <20°C (day) and <15°C (night)
- Pollination failure risk: <10°C (Hass) or <13°C (Shepard) within 3 days of pollination
- Floral initiation trigger: ≤15°C with daylength ≤10 hours for ≥4 weeks
- Extreme heat tolerance (mature foliage): 40°C for up to 25 hours without visible damage

Mean Annual Temperature for Commercial Production:
- Optimal range: 16.5–22.0°C
- Upper limit for good fruit quality: 22.0°C (fruit size, oil content, internal quality decline above this)

Source: Ramirez-Gil et al. (2024); Carey & Deuter (Queensland DPI); Wolstenholme (2002)

================================================================================
IRRIGATION & WATER MANAGEMENT
================================================================================

Soil Water Tension Thresholds:
- Recommended irrigation trigger: −30 kPa at 30 cm soil depth
- Critical water stress begins: −50 kPa at 30 cm soil depth
- Soil water content threshold: 0.4 m³/m³ in 0–30 cm layer

Water Deficit Impact on Fruit:
- 10% water deficit: 5% yield reduction
- 25% water deficit: 10% yield reduction
- Fruit weight loss during dry period: ~10% (approximately 26.4 g per fruit)
- Impact timing: Most critical during early fruit development

Water Use Efficiency:
- Average unnecessary water use (traditional methods): 2,377 gallons/hectare above crop demand
- Precision irrigation potential savings: Up to 50% reduction possible

Source: Kaneko et al. (2026, MDPI Horticulturae); UC Cooperative Extension

================================================================================
PEST-RELATED YIELD LOSS
================================================================================

Persea Mite (Oligonychus perseae):
- Economic Injury Level (EIL): 17% Percent Leaf Area Damaged (PLAD)
- Mite density threshold: 178 motile mites per leaf
- Sampling location: Upper side of second vein left of midrib (UML2) = 18 mites
- Cumulative mite-days (CMDs) for yield loss: ~18,300 per leaf (Tenerife conditions)
- CMDs for no yield loss: ~17,000 per leaf

Leaf Damage vs. Yield Loss Relationship:
- 5% leaf damage: 0% yield loss
- 10% leaf damage: 0% yield loss
- 17% leaf damage: ~5% yield loss (threshold)
- 25% leaf damage: ~15% yield loss
- 35% leaf damage: ~30% yield loss

Post-Harvest Loss:
- Total post-harvest loss at producer level: 24.1%
  - Poor harvesting practices: 5.7%
  - Pests and diseases: 4.0%
  - Lack of storage/handling: 21.0%

Source: Torres et al. (2024, MDPI Agronomy); Assessment studies (Ethiopia, Tanzania)

================================================================================
CANOPY SIZE & LIGHT INTERCEPTION
================================================================================

Canopy Volume vs. Productivity:
- Canopy volume range: 10,000–40,000 m³/ha
- Optimal canopy volume: 30,000–35,000 m³/ha
- Maximum yield per hectare: ~22–23 tonnes/ha at optimal canopy volume

Light Interception Thresholds:
- Optimal light interception: 80–84%
- Yield plateaus/declines above: 84% light interception
- Relationship: Yield increases linearly with light interception up to 84%

Fruit Weight Response:
- Mean fruit weight decreases as light interception increases beyond optimal
- Relationship varies between seasons
- Trade-off: Higher total yield but smaller individual fruits at maximum light interception

Source: Wilkie et al. (2019, Journal of Horticultural Science and Biotechnology)

================================================================================
SOIL & ROOT DISTRIBUTION
================================================================================

Root Depth Distribution:
- Feeder roots (0–15 cm): 60% of active roots
- Active roots (0–60 cm): 90% of total active root system
- Roots below 60 cm: 10% (minimal contribution)
- Shallow root system characteristic: Requires consistent moisture and excellent drainage

Soil Requirements:
- Optimal soil pH: 5.5–6.5 (slightly acidic)
- Soil type tolerance: Well-draining loam to sandy loam preferred
- Drainage requirement: Free-draining soil essential; sensitive to waterlogging
- Root rot pathogen: Phytophthora cinnamomi (no avocado rootstock completely resistant)

Soil Moisture Management:
- Healthy tree needs: Moist but free-draining soil
- Stress from: Dry soil (top 6 inches) or waterlogged conditions
- Mounding/drainage: Essential in high rainfall coastal areas

Source: Carey & Deuter (Queensland DPI); Whiley & Winston (1987)

================================================================================
SUNLIGHT & PHOTOSYNTHESIS
================================================================================

Light Intensity Thresholds:
- High light intensity: >1000 µmol m⁻² s⁻¹
- Photoinhibition risk: High light combined with temperatures >30°C
- Heat stress effect: Reduces CO₂ assimilation rates and stomatal conductance
- Extreme heat (40°C for 25 hours): Significantly reduces CO₂ assimilation in young seedlings

Canopy Management for Light:
- Shading decreases avocado fruit growth
- Excessive canopy density reduces individual fruit weight
- Optimal balance: 80–84% light interception without excessive shading

Source: Sharma & Manjeet (2020); Shapira et al. (2021); Alon et al. (2022)

================================================================================
FLOWERING & POLLINATION
================================================================================

Flower Production & Conversion:
- Mature tree flower production: ~1 million flowers per tree
- Flowers reaching early fruit maturation: ~1,000 (0.1% conversion rate)

Flowering Patterns (Hass vs. Shepard):
- Hass: Type A flowering (female morning Day 1, male afternoon Day 2)
- Shepard: Type B flowering (female afternoon Day 1, male morning Day 2)
- Synchronization: Requires stable temperatures; rapid temperature changes disrupt timing

Type B Sensitivity to Cold:
- Flowering cycle disrupted: Temperatures <20°C (day) and <15°C (night)
- Type A (Hass) tolerance: Slightly lower temperatures tolerated during flowering

Floral Initiation:
- Temperature requirement: ≤15°C with daylength ≤10 hours
- Duration needed: At least 4 weeks of cold treatment
- Result: Promotes "Fuerte" and "Hass" flowering in cool climates

Source: Salazaar-Garcia (1999); Lomas (1992); Hormaza et al. (2024)

================================================================================
FRUIT GROWTH & SIZE
================================================================================

Fruit Size Determinants:
- Final fruit size: Closely correlated with seed size
- Cell development: Fruit size determined by cell number rather than cell size
- Initial fruit size: Significant predictor of final fruit weight
- Growth rate: Varies seasonally; relationship between initial size and subsequent growth varies by season

Fruit Growth Phases:
- Maturation on tree: Fruit matures on tree but does not ripen until harvested
- Harvest timing flexibility: Allows growers to hold crop on tree, reducing harvest criticality
- Ripening trigger: Occurs only after harvest (ethylene-dependent)

Source: Cowan et al. (2001, Physiologia Plantarum); Avocadosource research

