PRODUCTION OVERVIEW (AUSTRALIA 2023-24)
================================================================================

Regional Production Distribution:
- Queensland: 64,000 tonnes from 11,000 hectares (53% of national production)
- Western Australia: 43,000 tonnes from 4,700 hectares (32% of national production)
- New South Wales: 12,000 tonnes (11% of national production)
- Other regions: <2% combined

Variety Distribution:
- Hass: 82% of annual production
- Shepard: 15% of annual production (early season, February–April in North Queensland)
- Other varieties: 3% (Maluma, Reed, Carmen, Lamb, Wurtz, Gem, Fuerte, Turner, Sharwil, Gwen, etc.)

Export Market (2023-24):
- Hong Kong: 42.1% of exports
- Singapore: 23.6% of exports
- Malaysia: 18.5% of exports
- Other destinations: 15.8% (UAE, Japan, etc.)
- Total exports: 14% of national production

Source: Avocados Australia; Australian Horticultural Statistics Handbook (2022-23)

================================================================================
SATELLITE DATA & REMOTE SENSING
================================================================================

NDVI (Normalized Difference Vegetation Index) for Yield Prediction:
- Prediction accuracy: R² = 0.70–0.85 when measured during fruit set period
- Optimal measurement timing: Fruit set phase (critical for yield determination)
- Application: Can predict avocado yield with moderate to high confidence

Remote Sensing Resolution Requirements:
- Individual tree canopy distinction: 0.3–0.5 m spatial resolution required
- Health assessment accuracy: High-resolution imagery (0.3–0.5 m) needed for accurate tree-level analysis
- Coarser resolution: Suitable for regional-scale production estimates only

Source: Remote sensing agriculture research; satellite imagery studies

================================================================================
CLIMATE CHANGE & FUTURE SUITABILITY
================================================================================

Temperature Range Adaptability:
- Avocado has highest suitable temperature range among tropical crops studied
- Current optimal range: 16.5–22°C mean annual temperature
- Climate vulnerability: Increasing temperatures and water scarcity threaten productivity
- Adaptation measures: Irrigation management, variety selection, canopy management

Source: Grüter et al. (2022, PLOS ONE); Climate change and agricultural workers study

